      },
      "block_order": [
        "column_fawpQF",
        "column_zkF9gA"
      ],
      "name": "Inositol science",
      "settings": {
        "contained": true,
        "max_width": 980,
        "heading": "<p>Your blood sugar looks normal. Your insulin isn't.</p>",
        "subheading": "<p>When you eat, insulin tells your cells to take the sugar out of your blood and use it for energy. <strong>Inositol is what carries that message inside the cell.</strong> When cells stop listening, your body sends more insulin to get the same job done. Glucose stays in range. Insulin climbs. A fasting glucose test never sees it.</p>",
        "caption": "Same knock. One of them gets answered.",
        "bottom_text": "<p>Estrogen helps your cells hear insulin. As it drops through perimenopause, they hear it less, so your body knocks louder. <strong>High insulin holds onto fat around your middle and drives the afternoon cravings.</strong> Unstable blood sugar overnight is one reason so many women wake at 3am.</p>",
        "page_bg": "#f2eeea",
        "bg_blur": 40,
        "overlay_color": "#f2eeea",
        "overlay_opacity": 45,
        "panel_bg": "#fbfaf9",
        "panel_border": "#d9d9e8",
