      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "Why it gets harder after 40",
        "heading": "Normal blood sugar. <em>Rising</em> insulin.",
        "heading_size": 0,
        "text": "<p>Your blood sugar can test normal for years while insulin quietly rises to keep it there. Standard labs check the sugar, not the insulin, so the first sign is usually how you feel.</p><p>As estrogen falls through perimenopause, cells respond less to insulin and the body makes more of it. <strong>Metabolic syndrome, the cluster insulin resistance drives, affects about 7% of adults in their 20s and over 40% by their 60s.*</strong></p><p>Same food, same walks, same sleep. Different results. That isn’t your discipline slipping.</p>",
        "body_size": 16,
        "button_label": "",
        "button_link": "",
        "chart_side": "right",
        "chart_title": "Metabolic syndrome by age, US adults",
        "chart_subtitle": "Share of adults meeting the criteria. Insulin resistance is the common thread.",
        "chart_style": "bars",
        "value_suffix": "%",
        "y_max": 50,
        "x_label": "Age",
        "band_from": "",
        "band_to": "",
        "band_label": "",
        "callout_index": 1,
        "callout_text": "Over 4 in 10 by their 60s",
        "source": "<p>Source: Ford ES, Giles WH, Dietz WH. Prevalence of the metabolic syndrome among US adults (NHANES III). <em>JAMA</em> 2002;287:356–359. Metabolic syndrome is the cluster of waist size, triglycerides, HDL, blood pressure and fasting glucose that insulin resistance drives. Figures are for all US adults, men and women. These statements have not been evaluated by the Food and Drug Administration. This product is not intended to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent any disease.</p>",
        "bg": "#f2eeea",
